{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* section of his biography states that he was born around 1140 and became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. \nHe was related to the royal family of Jerusalem through his mother who was the daughter of Baldwin II of Jerusalem and the sister of Melisende of Jerusalem and Alice of Antioch. \n*Reign* section of his biography mentions that Raymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the reign of his cousin Amalric of Jerusalem. \nHe was a key figure in the defense of the kingdom against the attacks of Nur ad-Din the ruler of Damascus and Aleppo. \n*Relationship with Byzantium* section of his biography states that Raymond III also had relations with the Byzantine Empire and was married to Hodierna's niece who was a member of the Byzantine royal family. \nHe was a skilled military leader and led the army of Tripoli to several victories against the Muslim forces. \n*Imprisonment and Later Life* section of his biography mentions that Raymond III was captured by the Muslims in 1164 and was imprisoned for several years before being released. \nAfter his release he continued to play an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a key figure in the defense of the kingdom against the attacks of Saladin. \n*Legacy* section of his biography states that Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Raymond of Antioch who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. \nHe is remembered as one of the most important figures in the history of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and a key figure in the defense of the kingdom against the Muslim forces. \nHe also played 